<h2>Storm Season in Temple and Bell County</h2>
<p>Temple sits along the I-35 corridor between Waco and Austin, an area that sees an active spring severe weather season, roughly March through June, followed by a secondary round of storms in the fall. Supercells moving across Bell County can drop hail in one neighborhood and spare the next, so it's common for one side of Temple to see heavy damage while homes a few miles away are untouched. Following the <a href="/weather/">local weather updates</a> after a storm can help you gauge whether your area was in the path.</p>
<p>Because many homes are affected at the same time, storms also bring out-of-town crews who may be gone by the time a problem surfaces. Take your time, ask for photos and a written estimate, and be cautious of anyone who pressures you to sign immediately or offers to cover your insurance deductible. Under Texas law, contractors cannot waive or absorb a deductible.</p>

<h2>When to Get a Post-Storm Inspection</h2>
<p>Schedule an inspection after any storm that brought hail to your area, produced strong gusts, or left debris in your yard, even if you see no leaks. Hail bruising and broken shingle seals often go unnoticed until the next heavy rain. Many insurance policies also have time limits for reporting damage, so an early inspection keeps your options open. If you plan to file a claim, our <a href="/services/roof-insurance-claim-assistance-temple-tx/">insurance claim assistance</a> page explains how we can help with documentation and estimates, and our article on <a href="/blog/roof-insurance-claims-texas-hailstorm/">roof insurance claims after a Texas hailstorm</a> covers the general process.</p>
